#{ WHY THERE ARE NO EXCEPTIONS -- coverage is testimony (R-44)
   ==========================================================

   Most languages let trouble travel invisibly: an exception thrown three
   calls down unwinds through callers that never named it, and lands --
   or does not -- somewhere far from the site that provoked it. The
   author of the middle layers testified to nothing; the language let
   them stay silent.

   This language refuses the silent path. A work declares its possible
   outcomes as a CLOSED set -- the signals: section of its panel -- and
   the AUTHOR OF EVERY CALL TESTIFIES AWARENESS of that set by writing a
   reaction to each outcome (12.9). The else:/elsewhile: block is that
   testimony: one arm per reckoned outcome. An outcome with no arm is
   not a runtime surprise; it is a COMPILE ERROR -- a failure to
   testify.

   Because every outcome is met at the site that provoked it, by an
   author who named it, there is NO invisible propagation path -- and so
   there is nothing for an 'exception' to be. A work reporting trouble
   upward signals with one of its OWN variants (12.10): every level
   speaks of malfunction in its own vocabulary, and every level's author
   has signed for what they pass on.

   THE ARM IS A CAUSALITY (R-44). The reaction reads like any reaction:
   '<variant> [when: <guard>] => <action>', first-match down the list,
   the fault's payload read through 'e.<field>' (names from the callee's
   declaration). One form for "when this happens, do that" -- whether
   the happening arrives on a channel (a causality in a behavior) or
   returns from a call (an arm in the testimony block).

   ~ANY -- TESTIMONY BY EXPLICIT BLANKET. Enumerating every variant can
   be needless where the response is uniform. '~ANY => <action>' is the
   author saying "and for every remaining outcome, THIS" -- coverage by
   a signed blanket clause, never by omission. Under ~ANY, 'e' IS
   NOTHING: an author who wants the details names the variant. An
   unguarded ~ANY ends the list -- first-match makes anything after it
   dead (checked).

   Below: 'measure' can end three ways. The caller answers 'too_small'
   specifically (reading the payload through e.), blankets the rest with
   ~ANY, and the plain path binds. Nothing can escape; nothing needs to.
}
measure : work(in: v
               out: m
               signals: too_small(gap), too_large(over), broken) {
    if: v < 10  { signal too_small(10 - v); }
    if: v > 90  { signal too_large(v - 90); }
    if: v == 13 { signal broken; }
    m = v * 2;
    give m;
}
Demo : reactor(in: cmd) {
    got: float; shortfall: float; odd: float;
    show : behavior {
        SHOW => {
            .got = measure(40) else: { ~ANY => ; }
            .shortfall = measure(3) else: {
                too_small => { .shortfall = e.gap; }
                ~ANY      => { .shortfall = 0 - 1; }
            }
            .odd = measure(95) else: {
                too_small => { .odd = e.gap; }
                ~ANY      => { .odd = 0 - 1; }
            }
        }
    }
}
plant : work() {
    f = script("SHOW");
    d = Demo();
    f ----> d;
    give;
}
